Why we built ARIA

Let me ask you a question. Think about the best developer on your team. The person who knows the product inside and out. The person everyone goes to when they are stuck.

Now think about what happens when that person is on vacation. Or when they move to a new team. Or when they leave the company. All that knowledge — years of it — walks out the door with them. The rest of the team is left to figure things out on their own.

This is a real problem. Today, so much of our knowledge lives inside the heads of a few people. Product knowledge. Coding best practices. Lessons learned the hard way. It is all there, but it is locked up. New team members take months to learn it. And every team keeps solving the same problems again and again.

This is the problem ARIA solves.

The idea behind ARIA is simple but powerful. We take the knowledge that lives with a few people, and we turn it into skills that everyone can use. Not a document that sits in a folder. Not a wiki page no one reads. A living, working skill backed inside an ARIA agent.

Here is what that means in real life. A person has deep knowledge built over many years. With ARIA, we capture that knowledge and turn it into a skill. Now, any developer — new or experienced — can ask ARIA and get the same guidance, the same context, and the same advice, at any time of day. It is like having that expert sitting next to you, whenever you need them.

What ARIA can do today

Let me give you a few simple examples of ARIA skills we have right now.

First, localization. If your app needs to support different languages and regions, ARIA can add that support for you the right way, the first time.

Second, REST APIs. ARIA can help you build APIs that follow RFC standards. No guessing, no shortcuts. Just clean, standard work.

Third, error handling. ARIA helps every service return error messages in the same clear format, based on RFC standards. So no matter which team built the service, the errors look and feel the same.

These are the same best practices we already follow in every development cycle. The difference is, now you do not have to remember them or look them up. ARIA carries them for you.

The ARIA Skills Marketplace

Now, a skill is only useful if people can find it. So we built one more thing: the ARIA Skills Marketplace.

Think of it like an app store, but for knowledge. It is one central place where every developer at PNC can go and see what is available.

In the marketplace, you can do five things.

One — you can discover skills. Browse what is out there and see what might help you today.

Two — you can learn from other teams. The knowledge one team shares becomes something every team can use.

Three — you can give feedback and ratings. If a skill helped you, say so. If it can be better, tell us. Every rating makes the skills stronger.

Four — you can add your own skills. And it is easy. You do not need to be an AI expert. If you know something valuable, you can share it with very little effort.

Five — and this is my favorite — you can reuse what already works. Why build something from scratch when another team has already solved it? Take it, use it, and spend your time on new problems instead.

